<Routines>
<Routine Name="Logic" Type="RLL">
<Description>
<![CDATA[Calculate tank's Gross Observed Volume (GOV) based on level, temperature, calibration table]]>
</Description>
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Copyright © Rockwell Automation, Inc.  All Rights Reserved.
============================================================
P_StrapTbl:  PROCESS -- TANK STRAPPING TABLE
============================================================
Revision 3.1-01 Release  2014-09-03:  See V3.1-xx Release Notes for details.
V2.x Revision History archived.
V3.0-00  2013-06-30: Added Inf_Lib. Added EU String for Temperature
V3.1-00  2014-06-30:  Unmodified for Library of Process Objects V3.1, up-rev only
V3.1-01  2014-09-03:  Unmodified, up-rev only for V3.1-01 public release.
============================================================
This instruction calculates the Gross Observed Volume (GOV) of Product in
an upright cylindrical tank, given:

    Input: Overall Level (Innage, from bottom ref.) (typ. Product + Free Water
      - see below)
    Configuration: Tank Calibration Table (array with volume at given level)
(also called a Strapping Table, hence the instruction name)

If optional Free Water adjusment is selected, the following are required:

    Input: Interface Innage Level (level of Free Water only)

If optional Compensation for Thermal Expansion of the tank shell
is selected, the following are required:

    Input: Average Product Temperature  (suggestion: use P_AInMulti ! )
    Input: Ambient Temperature (if tank is not insulated)
    Configuration: "K" factor (weighting of product vs ambient)
       (enter zero for this configuration if tank is insulated)
    Configuration: Linear Coefficient of Thermal Expansion for tank shell material of construction
    Configuration:  Reference Temperature for tank calibration table (typ. 60 Deg F or 15 Deg C)

If optional Floating Roof Adjustment is selected, the following are required:

    Input:  Obesrved Density (Deg API) at PRODUCT Temperature
    Configuration:  Reference Density (typ. 35 API)
    Configuration:  Correction Volume per API (typ. a negative number)
    Configuration:  Level at which to start FRA (level above which roof floats)

The Gross Observed Volume (GOV) and intermediates are provided as Values:
    Val_TOV:  Total Observed Volume
    Val_FW:    Free Water Volume
    Val_CTSh:  Correction for Temperature of Tank Shell (multiplier)
    Val_FRA:   Floating Roof Adjustment (volume)
    Val_GOV:  Primary Value: Gross Observed Volume

Val_GOV is provided without correction for product temperautre, density,
included sediment and water (BS&W).  Those corrections depend on the
product, not the tank, and are beyond the scope of this instruction.

References:  API MPMS 2.2A, API MPMS 12.1.1;  see www.api.org
for more information.
============================================================
SHOW LOCAL STRINGS
============================================================
The STRINGs containing the text associated with each instance of this
instruction cannot be Input or Output Parameters, because STRINGs are not
 'atomic' types. (Inputs and Outputs must be SINT, INT, DINT, REAL, or BOOL.)

In order to make it easier to find and configure these STRINGs, this rung
was added.  THE JMP (and the LBL on the following rung)
MUST NOT BE REMOVED!!!

To view the STRINGs, go to the instruction instance (in LD or FBD), RIGHT-click
to bring up the context menu and select "Open Instruction Logic".
This rung will appear with the STRING values for the selected instance.
You may double-click the STRING values here to modify them as well.

#####  V3.0-00  2013-06-30:  Added instruction to show EU strings  #####]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[JMP(SkipText)CONCAT(Cfg_Desc,Cfg_Label,Cfg_Tag)CONCAT(Cfg_LevelEU,Cfg_VolumeEU,Cfg_TempEU)LOWER(Inf_Lib,Inf_Type);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
GET TABLE SIZE (ROWS)
============================================================
This rung determines the number of rows in the configured Strapping Table.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[LBL(SkipText)SIZE(Cfg_CalTbl,0,Wrk_FSC.LEN);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
GIVEN OVERALL LEVEL, FIND TOTAL OBSERVED VOLUME (TOV)
============================================================
Look up the input overall level (and interpolate)
in the tank calibration table.

This rung searches the strapping table for the first entry where the level
in the entry is greater than the given input level.

(Use major and minor units, e.g., feet and inches or meters and cm, if available,
or just use major unit (feet, meters) if configured for no minor units.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[RES(Wrk_FSC)[GRT(Cfg_MinorPerMajor,0.0) FSC(Wrk_FSC,?,?,ALL,Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_FSC.POS].Minor/Cfg_MinorPerMajor+Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_FSC.POS].Major>Inp_Level) JMP(CalcRTV) ,FSC(Wrk_FSC,?,?,ALL,Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_FSC.POS].Major>Inp_Level) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="3" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If the level is above the highest calibrated level, return the highest
calibrated volume and flag the value as OVER MAX.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[LBL(CalcRTV)XIO(Wrk_FSC.FD)OTE(Sts_OverMax)SUB(Wrk_FSC.LEN,1,Wrk_N)MOV(Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N].Volume,Val_TOV);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="4" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If the level is below the lowest calibrated level, return the lowest
calibrated volume (e.g., zero) and flag the value as UNDER MIN.

Otherwise, the level is within the calibration table.  Find the entries
that "surround" the input level and interpolate to get the
RAW TOTAL VOLUME (product and water).]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Wrk_FSC.FD)[EQU(Wrk_FSC.POS,0) OTE(Sts_UnderMin) MOV(Cfg_CalTbl[0].Volume,Val_TOV) ,GRT(Wrk_FSC.POS,0) [MOV(Wrk_FSC.POS,Wrk_N) SUB(Wrk_FSC.POS,1,Wrk_N_1) ,GRT(Cfg_MinorPerMajor,0.0) CPT(Wrk_Level_N,Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N].Major+Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N].Minor/Cfg_MinorPerMajor) CPT(Wrk_Level_N_1,Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N_1].Major+Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N_1].Minor/Cfg_MinorPerMajor) ,LEQ(Cfg_MinorPerMajor,0.0) CPT(Wrk_Level_N,Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N].Major) CPT(Wrk_Level_N_1,Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N_1].Major) ,CPT(Val_TOV,(Inp_Level-Wrk_Level_N_1)/(Wrk_Level_N-Wrk_Level_N_1)*(Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N].Volume-Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N_1].Volume)+Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N_1].Volume) ] ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="5" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
GIVEN FREE WATER LEVEL, FIND FREE WATER ADJUSTMENT
============================================================
Look up the input free waterl level (and interpolate)
in the tank calibration table.

This rung searches the strapping table for the first entry where the level
in the entry is greater than the given input free water level.

(Use major and minor units, e.g., feet and inches or meters and cm, if available,
or just use major unit (feet, meters) if configured for no minor units.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[RES(Wrk_FSC)[GRT(Cfg_MinorPerMajor,0.0) FSC(Wrk_FSC,?,?,ALL,Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_FSC.POS].Minor/Cfg_MinorPerMajor+Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_FSC.POS].Major>Inp_FWLevel) JMP(CalcFW) ,FSC(Wrk_FSC,?,?,ALL,Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_FSC.POS].Major>Inp_FWLevel) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="6" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If the level is above the highest calibrated level, return the highest
calibrated volume and flag the value as OVER MAX.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[LBL(CalcFW)XIO(Wrk_FSC.FD)SUB(Wrk_FSC.LEN,1,Wrk_N)MOV(Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N].Volume,Val_FW);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="7" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If the level is below the lowest calibrated level, return the lowest
calibrated volume (e.g., zero) and flag the value as UNDER MIN.

Otherwise, the level is within the calibration table.  Find the entries
that "surround" the input level and interpolate to get the
RAW TOTAL VOLUME (product and water).]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(Wrk_FSC.FD)[EQU(Wrk_FSC.POS,0) MOV(Cfg_CalTbl[0].Volume,Val_FW) ,GRT(Wrk_FSC.POS,0) [MOV(Wrk_FSC.POS,Wrk_N) SUB(Wrk_FSC.POS,1,Wrk_N_1) ,GRT(Cfg_MinorPerMajor,0.0) CPT(Wrk_Level_N,Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N].Major+Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N].Minor/Cfg_MinorPerMajor) CPT(Wrk_Level_N_1,Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N_1].Major+Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N_1].Minor/Cfg_MinorPerMajor) ,LEQ(Cfg_MinorPerMajor,0.0) CPT(Wrk_Level_N,Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N].Major) CPT(Wrk_Level_N_1,Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N_1].Major) ,CPT(Val_FW,(Inp_FWLevel-Wrk_Level_N_1)/(Wrk_Level_N-Wrk_Level_N_1)*(Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N].Volume-Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N_1].Volume)+Cfg_CalTbl[Wrk_N_1].Volume) ] ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="8" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
CORRECTION FOR TEMPERATURE OF TANK SHELL
============================================================
Calculate the multiplier for the volume based on the
thermal expansion of the tank shell.

Note these linear thermal expansion coefficients for various
tank materials of construction:

Mild Carbon Steel:   6.2E-6 / Deg F  or  1.12E-5 / Deg C
304 Stainless:    9.6E-6 / Deg F  or  1.73E-5 / Deg C
316 Stainless:    8.83E-6 / Deg F   or   1.59E-5 / Deg C
17-4PH Stainless:   6.0E-6 / Deg F   or   1.08E-5 / Deg C

The shell temperature of an insulated tank is assumed to be
equal to the product temperature.

The shell temperature of an uninsulated tank is based on a weighted 
average of the product temperature and the ambient temperature.
The "weight" is adjustable based on the particular installation, but a value of
7.0 ( 7 x product temp +  1 x ambient temp, divided by 8 ) is commonly used
and is the default for this instruction.

Enter a weight of zero if the tank is insulated, and only the product
temperature will be used for the shell temperature.

CTSh = 1.0 + 2.0**a*((T-Tref) + a**2*(T-Tref)

(T, the shell temperature, and Tref, the shell reference
temperature from the Strapping Table, are in the same units
as the linear thermal expansion coefficient...)
]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIO(Cfg_HasCTSh) MOV(Cfg_CalTemp,Val_TSh) CLR(Wrk_TDiff) MOV(1.0,Val_CTSh) ,XIC(Cfg_HasCTSh) [EQU(Cfg_K,0.0) MOV(Inp_AvgProdTemp,Val_TSh) ,NEQ(Cfg_K,0.0) CPT(Val_TSh,(Inp_AvgProdTemp*Cfg_K+Inp_AmbTemp)/(Cfg_K+1.0)) ,SUB(Val_TSh,Cfg_CalTemp,Wrk_TDiff) ,CPT(Val_CTSh,1.0+(2.0*Cfg_ShellCoefOfExp*Wrk_TDiff)+(Cfg_ShellCoefOfExp*Cfg_ShellCoefOfExp*Wrk_TDiff*Wrk_TDiff)) ] ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="9" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
CALCULATE FLOATING ROOF ADJUSTMENT
============================================================
If the overall level is at or above the level at which the roof floats
(it's up off its supports), and if the Floating Roof Adjustment
option is selected, calculate a volume adjustment based
on the roof floating on a different density liquid.

NOTE:  Inp_ObsAPI must be the API AT THE PRODUCT TEMPERATURE
(not at standard temperature - 60 Deg F or 15 Deg C)]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIC(Cfg_HasFRA) GEQ(Inp_Level,Cfg_FloatRoofLevel) CPT(Val_FRA,Cfg_FloatRoofVolPerAPI*(Inp_ObsAPI-Cfg_FloatRoofCalAPI)) ,XIO(Cfg_HasFRA) CLR(Val_FRA) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="10" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[============================================================
CALCULATE FINAL GROSS OBSERVED VOLUME
============================================================
GOV =  [ ( TOV - FW ) x CTSh ] + FRA]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[CPT(Val_GOV,((Val_TOV-Val_FW)*Val_CTSh)+Val_FRA);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
</RLLContent>
</Routine>
</Routines>
<Dependencies>
<Dependency Type="DataType" Name="P_StrapTblRow"/>
<Dependency Type="DataType" Name="STRING_40"/>
<Dependency Type="DataType" Name="STRING_20"/>
<Dependency Type="DataType" Name="STRING_8"/>
<Dependency Type="DataType" Name="STRING_12"/>
<Dependency Type="DataType" Name="STRING_16"/>
</Dependencies>
</AddOnInstructionDefinition>
</AddOnInstructionDefinitions>
</Controller>
</RSLogix5000Content>
